--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
TABLE 0 WAS AN APPARATUS FAILURE, PRESERVED AT t0_apparatus_failure/ RATHER THAN DELETED.

The driver was DERIVED BY SED from #521's, and the substitution list missed one line: the
hardcoded path to that round's MUTATOR.  So every leg called the WRONG TOOL, which replied
`unknown mutant M_ORIG' and exited 9.

*** THE HARD-FAIL DID ITS JOB -- the driver refused rather than building and measuring an
unmutated tree and reporting it as a kill. ***  That is the MUTANT-TEXT-NOT-FOUND rule
paying for itself a third time in two rounds (an ambiguous anchor and a
comment-for-statement in #521, a wrong tool here).

*** BUT THE SHAPE IS NEW AND WORTH NAMING: THE TABLE LOOKED HALF-HEALTHY, NOT DEAD. ***
BASE scored 19/19 and RESTORED scored 19/19, because **BOTH mutators define a mutant called
BASE** and both answer it the same way -- "baseline installed unmutated".  The two tools
share part of their namespace, so calling the wrong one produced a real-looking score on
the two legs that bracket the table and blanks in between.

  A SUMMARY WITH TWO GOOD SCORES AND FOUR BLANKS READS LIKE A RUN THAT PARTLY WORKED.  It
  was a run that entirely did not.  The discriminator is that a BLANK is not a score: this
  project's rule is that an unrun check and a failed one must not render the same, and here
  four blanks in a score column were the only signal that anything was wrong.

  THE CHEAP GUARD, and it is the denominator rule again: a table driver should assert that
  every leg produced a score before it prints a summary, rather than printing whatever it
  found.  Filed as a tooling note rather than built here -- it is a change to a driver
  shared by future rounds and owes its own controls.

Nothing was measured in table 0, so no number from it is cited anywhere.  Table 1 below is
the first real run.

