TABLE 1 (SCORES.txt).  ZERO ESCAPES.

  BASE      19/19
  M_ORIG    14/19   zx32, rot8, vac neighbour, census wrong, census zero
  M_NAIVE   14/19   zx32, rot8, vacuous cell, vac neighbour, census wrong
  M_MASKEND 16/19   zx32, vacuous cell, census wrong
  M_NOAND   15/19   zx32, rot8, vacuous cell, vac neighbour
  HEAD      14/19   as M_ORIG -- the whole-file null medium
  RESTORED  19/19   cmp-vs-SEC CLEAN, binary FRESH, sentinel cleared on both parts

*** THE CHANNEL BLOCK ABOVE WAS WRONG ABOUT ONE ROW, AND THE TABLE CORRECTED IT. ***
I declared `vacuous cell' as "NAMES NO MUTANT IT CAN KILL, DELIBERATELY", carried to show
where the vacuity boundary is and not counted as coverage.  **It reddens under THREE of
the five mutants** -- M_NAIVE, M_MASKEND and M_NOAND.

The claim was true of exactly ONE mutant and I generalised it to all of them.  That cell's
correct answer is zero, so it cannot see the ORIGINAL defect, whose symptom is also zero --
but every other mutant here makes it return something NON-zero, and the row then fires.

  **VACUITY IS A PROPERTY OF (ROW, MUTANT) PAIRS, NOT OF ROWS.**  A row is vacuous
  AGAINST A NAMED MUTANT, and this project's CHANNEL rule says exactly that -- "a row whose
  named channel cannot vary UNDER ITS NAMED MUTANT" -- while my block wrote the weaker,
  wrong sentence "names no mutant it can kill".  The same slip in the other direction cost
  #521 a floor breach, where I asserted a channel for two rows that turned out not to vary.
  Both errors are the same one: reasoning about a row in isolation instead of against a
  specific edit.

TWO RESULTS THAT WENT EXACTLY AS PREDICTED AND ARE WORTH KEEPING:
  * `census zero' reds under M_ORIG and HEAD AND NOWHERE ELSE.  That is precisely right --
    it is the row that turns "always zero" into a MEASURED universal, and only the
    always-zero forms trip it.  Under the other three mutants the answer is wrong but not
    zero, so the row correctly stays green and `census wrong' does the work instead.
  * M_MASKEND, the NULLED REPAIR, is the HARDEST mutant at 16/19 -- it keeps the corrected
    `tmp2 |=' and merely shifts the mask by one bit, so the result is non-zero and only
    three rows notice.  A detector built to catch "returns zero" and nothing else would
    pass it.  That is what clause (d) is for and it is discharged here rather than assumed.

*** AND M_NAIVE -- THE REPAIR THE PRODUCT COMMENT WARNS AGAINST -- IS KILLED AT 14/19. ***
The warning is now a tested claim rather than an assertion.  A future reader who takes that
edit reds five rows immediately.

