A HONEST NOTE ON TWO LEGS THAT COINCIDE, raised by the grok seat mid-answer during pass 2
and recorded before it finished, because the point stands on its own.

**HEAD AND M_ORIG SCORE IDENTICALLY -- 14/19 EACH, WITH THE SAME FIVE FAILING ROWS -- AND
THAT IS NOT A COINCIDENCE, IT IS A PROPERTY OF THIS ROUND.**

  HEAD    is the whole committed pre-fix FILE, the null medium for clause (c).
  M_ORIG  is the verbatim reinstatement of the removed defect, clause (b).

For a diff of ONE TOKEN in one function, those two media are THE SAME PROGRAM.  The
committed file differs from the fixed one only by that token, so installing the committed
file and reinstating the token produce byte-equivalent behaviour.

  *** SO CLAUSES (b) AND (c) ARE SATISFIED BY WHAT IS EFFECTIVELY ONE MEASUREMENT, AND THE
  ROUND MUST SAY SO RATHER THAN PRESENT TWO LEGS AS TWO INDEPENDENT CHECKS. ***

That is DISCLOSED rather than defended.  The two legs are still worth running separately --
they are produced by DIFFERENT MECHANISMS (a git checkout of the whole file versus a
literal text replacement), so running both cross-checks the mutator against the repository
and would catch a mutator that silently edited the wrong thing.  **That cross-check is what
the pair actually buys here, and it is a smaller claim than "the floor has two independent
witnesses".**

  Note the general shape: **a null medium and a verbatim reinstatement diverge only when
  the diff touches more than the one thing the reinstatement puts back.**  #521's diff was
  also one token and its HEAD and M_OLD legs likewise coincided at 16/57; nobody noticed
  there either.  For a round with a multi-part fix they would differ, and then the pair
  really would be two checks.

*Recorded from a seat's WORKING NOTES rather than its verdict, which is worth its own line:
this project's rule is to read the body of any seat whose verdict merely agrees, and here
the useful sentence appeared before there was a verdict at all.*

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PASS 2 CORRECTED THIS BLOCK TWICE (grok, RATIFY-with-residuals).  Both are corrections to
MY record rather than to the fix, and both are the same species: a claim about the WHOLE
input space that is true of most of it.

*** (1) "THE CENSUS WALKS THE CROSS-PRODUCT OF sh AND mb" -- OVERCLAIMED.  THE SH SWEEP
DOES NOT TEST SH. ***  The census drives an ALL-ONES source, deliberately, so that every
mask window is non-empty and "190 of 190" is a real number.  But with an all-ones source
ROTL64 IS THE IDENTITY -- so the 63 cells of the SH sweep all sit at MB=0, all want
all-ones, and are **63 copies of (0,0) for mask purposes**.

  **THE CENSUS PINS MASK(MB,63).  IT DOES NOT PIN THE ROTATE.**  The rotate is pinned by
  the two VALUE rows -- `zx32 rldicl' and `rot8 rldicl' -- which drive a source whose bits
  differ, and `rot8' in particular uses an all-ones MASK so that it is a PURE rotate.  That
  is adequate coverage and it is a smaller claim than the one this block made.  A round
  wanting a genuine two-axis census would need a second source value.

*** (2) "M_MASKEND IS OFF BY ONE BIT AT EVERY CELL" -- FALSE OF MB=0, AND THE MUTANT IS
ITSELF UNDEFINED. ***  `(63-mb)' -> `(62-mb)' evaluates `1 << -1' on the final iteration,
which is UB, not a clean shift.  Measured, `zx32' returned 0x800000004afef00d -- bit 63
WRAPPED IN.  And `census wrong' is 126, i.e. 190 - 64: **every MB=0 cell comes out
CORRECT**, because the wrap plus the shifted bits happen to fill all 64 bits.

  The mutant is still KILLED at 16/19 and clause (d) is discharged, so nothing about the
  floor changes.  **But a nulled-repair mutant that is itself undefined is sloppy**: it
  measures a compiler's treatment of UB rather than the repair's own weakening.  The clean
  form was `mb <= 62' -- weaken the LOOP BOUND, not the shift count.  Recorded rather than
  re-run, because the clause is discharged either way and re-tabling to make a mutant
  tidier is not a good use of the machine.

*** AND ONE RESIDUAL THAT IS A DISPOSITION RATHER THAN A CORRECTION: DO NOT "SAVE sh" HERE.
***  That repair belongs to the sibling row.  `rldicl' never re-reads `sh' after its rotate
loop, so saving it would be a change with no observable and no warrant -- the addition
licence refusing an edit that "cannot hurt".

