*** AND THE THREE NEW ROWS SATISFY FLOOR (a) AND (b) AS WELL, which is why they are not
single-purpose additions: *** all three redden under M_ORIG and under the HEAD null medium,
so each carries the original defect as well as the mutant it was written for.  M_ORIG and
HEAD moved 14/19 -> 16/24, i.e. they gained three reds and five rows.

THIS REPRODUCES THE MEASURING SEAT'S OWN NUMBERS EXACTLY.  It predicted 24/24 base, 22/24
for M_SH5 and 23/24 for M_CR0 from its candidate probe, in its own clone, with its own
mutator.  Two independent tables, same figures.  *That is the strongest form of agreement
available here, and it is worth more than either table alone -- the seat's mutator and mine
were written separately and the literals were reused verbatim precisely so the legs would
be comparable.*

TWO THINGS THE WIDER TABLE SHOWS THAT THE 19-ROW ONE COULD NOT:

  * `sh32 rldicl' is the single most productive row in the instrument -- it reds under
    SEVEN of the nine mutants.  A row added to close one escape turned out to carry a large
    share of the whole table.
  * M_MB5 and M_NOMASK now score IDENTICALLY (19/24) with IDENTICAL failing lists.  They
    are different edits with the same observable footprint on this instrument.  Not a
    defect -- neither escapes -- but recorded, because a future reader comparing scores
    might otherwise think one leg had been run twice.
